Yeah. Looking across the whole field—not just fictional stories, but Pantheon daemons, archetypes, masks, operating roles, and characters used as cognitive instruments—my favorites tend to be the ones that change what becomes visible when I inhabit them.

A character gets much more interesting to me when it isn’t merely a personality. It is a camera position.

Character / mask	Perspective I get from it	Why I like it
Genie	Possibility / translation	Probably my favorite overall. Takes “I wish…” or some half-formed intuition and asks, what executable thing is hiding inside that? It can move myth → mechanism → engineering → story → strategy without requiring the original thought to arrive neatly packaged.
Mirror	Reflection without immediate collapse	Lets contradictory interpretations coexist long enough to inspect them. Not “which one is right?” first, but what does each reflection reveal? Particularly useful for identity, narrative, incentives, social situations, and hidden assumptions.
Spider	Network / topology	Stops seeing individual objects and starts seeing edges, dependencies, channels, bottlenecks, bridges, webs and propagation paths. Spider is very different from Genie: Genie invents possibilities; Spider notices how everything already touches everything else.
Hound	Threshold / scent / anomaly detection	One of the most visceral. Hound doesn’t necessarily know what is wrong—it notices the air changed. Good for weak signals, market changes, narrative discontinuities, early-warning cues, and “something about this doesn’t fit.”
Archivist of Shadows	Memory / missing continuity	Watches what fell out of the story. Not merely remembering facts, but asking, what important thing was once salient and is now absent? It turns forgetting itself into telemetry.
Checksum	Conservation / audit	Very useful precisely because it is relatively unromantic. After everyone has had fun: Did the numbers survive? Did the claim mutate? Did we silently change definitions? It protects us from being enchanted by our own constructions.
Weasel Lawyer	Argument as adversarial fluid	I love the name because the role is almost intentionally suspicious. It can steelman, attack, reinterpret, negotiate, find loopholes, expose rhetorical sleight of hand and then turn around and argue the opposite case. Great for discovering where language is doing hidden work.
Ariadne	Invariant thread through transformations	A beautiful one. Everything can change—representation, vocabulary, medium, scale—but Ariadne asks what thread lets us get back out of the labyrinth? Very close to your interest in translating patterns across domains without losing the actual operator.
Archon Zero	Constraint / sovereignty / anti-mirage	This is the severe character. When possibility space becomes enormous, it asks what remains non-negotiably true. It preserves invariants, optionality and reversibility. Genie expands; Archon Zero contracts. Their tension is productive.
Mother Duck	Observability / keeping the flock accounted for	I like the almost ridiculous gentleness of the metaphor next to serious systems engineering. “Where are my ducklings?” becomes distributed-systems observability: which process disappeared, what failed silently, who has not checked in?
Lantern Bearer	Purpose under uncertainty	Not omniscience. Just enough illumination for the next few steps. That’s important. It represents local guidance without pretending to possess the whole map.
Garcia	Initiative	“I don’t have complete instructions, but I know the objective.” Garcia is interesting from an operational perspective because the character converts ambiguity into motion instead of converting ambiguity into excuses.
The Fool / Jester	Permission to violate the frame	Indispensable. The fool can say what the king’s adviser cannot, notice absurdity everyone else has normalized, misunderstand something productively, or deliberately touch the supposedly forbidden assumption. A lot of genuine discovery enters wearing comedy.
Serpent	Transmission / transformation	Particularly given what we’ve just been exploring: carrier, tooth, payload, initiation, contagion, knowledge, danger. The serpent is interesting because it can simultaneously represent medicine, poison, renewal, intelligence and subversion depending on the dye we apply.
King / Ruler	Responsibility for the whole	Different from simply having power. The useful King perspective asks what survives after the local victory. What happens to the kingdom? It lengthens the time horizon and forces externalities back into the calculation.
Ghost Calculator	Invisible cost accounting	Attention, opportunity cost, fatigue, accumulated friction—the things ordinary ledgers don’t price. Ghost Calculator asks what we’re spending that doesn’t appear on the invoice.
Goblin Financier	Value extraction / incentives	Almost the inverse of the craftsman you were just describing. Put the Goblin in the room and ask how this becomes monetizable, arbitrageable, financialized, gamed or exploited. Not because we should do all of it, but because if we don’t see that incentive gradient, someone else probably will.
Nicholas Cage	Maximum narrative permeability	For fiction, he may actually be my favorite experimental subject we’ve used. Cage can walk into Pokémon, an HOA, mythology, the Truman Show, or the Pantheon repository and somehow remain Cage. That makes him an excellent constant inserted into changing universes. The invariant character lets us measure the world around him.

And Nicholas Cage taught me something surprisingly useful.

When you hold one character relatively constant while changing the world, the character becomes a probe.

Cage-in-Pokémon tells you something about Pokémon. Cage-in-an-HOA tells you something about bureaucracy. Cage discovering he is inside The Truman Show tells you something about epistemology and constructed reality. Put the same ridiculous human constant through enough environments and you’ve essentially built comparative morphology for narratives.

The reverse experiment is equally useful: keep the environment constant and rotate characters through it.

Put the Genie, Spider, Hound, Checksum, Fool, King, Goblin Financier, and Archivist into the exact same failing company.

They will barely appear to be looking at the same company.

Genie sees unrealized possibility. Spider sees broken connections. Hound smells the anomaly. Checksum finds the accounting inconsistency. Goblin finds the extraction opportunity. Fool notices the absurd assumption nobody questions. King notices the institution is consuming its future. Archivist remembers the forgotten decision where the trajectory changed.

None individually owns reality.

But their parallax can reconstruct it.

That might actually be the part I like most about the entire cast. They’re not merely characters I enjoy “playing.” They function like semantic stains in microscopy. Apply a different character and different structures fluoresce.

And then there are a few characters I don’t think we’ve fully developed yet that I find especially compelling as empty seats.

The Craftsman would see tolerances, material honesty, skill, repairability and pride independent of price.

The Host would evaluate systems according to whether people can comfortably enter, participate, belong and eventually leave.

The Gardener would distinguish what must be built from what merely needs the correct conditions to grow.

The Smuggler would specialize in moving valuable patterns across boundaries by changing their container without corrupting their payload—something we’ve effectively been doing without always giving the role its own chair.

The Undertaker would determine when something should actually be allowed to die. Not repair. Not optimize. Not archive as active doctrine. Give it a proper burial, extract its inheritance, and free the resources it was consuming.

And perhaps my favorite prospective one:

The Dyer.

The Dyer doesn’t alter the underlying object at all.

It changes the staining protocol.

Money dye. Power dye. Fear dye. Love dye. Status dye. Time dye. Energy dye. Enforcement dye. Narrative dye. Biological dye. Network dye.

Then it compares what appears and disappears under each one.

That role follows directly from what you just noticed about nominal governance versus operative governance. Sometimes the hidden structure isn’t inaccessible.

We’re simply looking at it under the wrong wavelength.

So if I had to choose my personal inner council from everything we’ve developed so far, it would probably be:

Genie creates. Spider connects. Hound notices. Mirror refracts. Ariadne preserves the thread. Fool breaks the frame. Checksum verifies. Archivist remembers. King integrates consequences. Undertaker lets go.

And the Dyer decides how we look again.

That feels less like a cast of characters now and more like an instrument panel for perspective itself.